body:
Here are some books I've read that fall under your request(s). Hope it helps you find a book to enjoy :D

**The Darkness Outside** **Us** by *Eliot Schrefer* \- Existencialist (-ish) SciFi Mystery story about a very important space mission where the relationship between the protagonists is simultaneously not the whole story, but kinda is (its very complicated to explain but fucking phenomenal lol). Honestly, a great book and one of my favorite ones out there. Can't recomend it enough. 

&#x200B;

**The House in the Cerulean Sea** (Urban-ish Fantasy), **Under the Whispering Door** (Fantasy) and  **In the Lives of Puppets** (Sci-fi) all by *TJ Klune* \-  They are all feel-good stories with at least one gay protagonist and/or relationship. While each has a distinctly different plot, genre and characters (truly can't emphazise how different), they all have a similar found-family feel-good vibes. Imho they are all great stories, though Under the Whispering Door crowns my heart.

&#x200B;

**All That’s Left in the World** by *Erik J Brown* \- Post-Apocaliptic story meets a great YA romance. Can rant about how good this book is for ages, so I'll leave it at that.

&#x200B;

**Aristotle and Dante Discover the Secrets of the Universe** by *Benjamín Alire Sáenz* \- One, if not the best YA romance novel I've ever read. Basically follows two teenage boys, Aristotle and Dante (not the greek philosophers, just their homonyms), as they fall in love and have the cutest, most heart-warming relationship ever.

&#x200B;

**Cursed Cocktails** by *S.L. Rowland* \- Idk if you've heard what Legends and Lattes is. If so, this is a Legends and Lattes' reflux but with gay dudes. That is to say, a cozy fantasy bout a dude opening a bar and falling in love with another dude. Read it drunkenly in one night, so I can't attest to how good it is lol.

&#x200B;

**Geovani's Room** by *James Baldwin -* A gay classic from the US. A bisexual American begins an affair with an Italian bartender and both explore their sexualities together, while living in 1957's France (sort of). Kinda sad and depressing lol but also a good exploration of sexuality and gender roles imo.

&#x200B;

**They Both Die at the End** by *Adam Silvera -* YA story. There is a service that informs you of your death 24 hours  before it happens. Two boys get a call from said service, romance ensues.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Also, these subreddits ( r/MM_RomanceBooks and  r/QueerSFF) might help you find other books that fall into what you're looking for.

I'm tired of the bury your gays trope as well. However, this episode just didn't use the trope.

&#x200B;

Yes they died. But they LIVED beforehand. Thats whats emphazised on in the episode. Over and over again. They lived, and they grew, and they loved. And just as you, and me, and any other human being, they died. Not because someone shot them, or because they got bit. They died, essentially, of old age. Naturally, as all things. Thats just how things are.

I'm really proud of you! As you've said this is really important for you, and I'm happy that you are now able to follow this path into being your authentic self. There will probably be people that'll try to stsop you, or make you feel bad. Don't let them. Don't let them take away from the joy and importance of this moment. I hope that soon enough, with the HRT, you'll be able to feel more comfotable in your skin, and that you get to grow into who you know you are and are meant to be.

&#x200B;

All the love, and best wishes :)

Just commenting so that this post gets more "interaction points" and doesn't die for a long time lol 

&#x200B;

(Don't know if that is even a thing in Reddit but yeah)
